+<p>No. A scene holds attention when something important is at stake, even if the characters remain seated and speak quietly. A discovery, a change of allegiance or a question left unanswered can be as gripping as a chase. The writer&#x27;s task is to give each development the time it needs.</p>
 <h2>Why does quiet drama hold our attention?</h2>
 <p>In <i>Elementary</i>, much of the pleasure lies in watching Holmes and Watson notice, question and reason. A small inconsistency can overturn an entire theory. The mystery gives viewers something to solve alongside the characters. There may be a body, a pursuit or a confrontation, but those moments do not have to dominate the episode.</p>
 <p>Quiet drama also gives dialogue room for precision and humour. People who listen carefully, speak eloquently and disagree without shouting can be fascinating company. Calm delivery does not diminish the consequences of what is being said.</p>
 <h2>What actually makes a scene dramatic?</h2>
-<p>A useful test comes from David Mamet's memo to the writers of <i>The Unit</i>: identify what a character wants, what happens if they do not get it, and why the matter must be dealt with now.</p>
+<p>A useful test comes from David Mamet&#x27;s memo to the writers of <i>The Unit</i>: identify what a character wants, what happens if they do not get it, and why the matter must be dealt with now.</p>
 <p>Imagine a detective asking a witness about a missing hour. The witness has an explanation. The detective knows one detail that contradicts it but does not immediately reveal what she knows. Both characters have an objective, and every answer changes their position. The tension comes from the contest between them, not the volume of their voices.</p>
-<h1>When should a scene be allowed to breathe?</h1>
-<p>Script consultant Justine Hart recently described the absence of what she calls 'settle-in scenes' in some spec scripts. Her observation is useful when applied to relationships. A friendship cannot convincingly disintegrate merely because the plot requires it. We may need to see the small concessions, evasions and discoveries that bring it to breaking point.</p>
-<p>Allowing time for that progression is different from retaining repetitive exchanges. Each part of the scene should alter our understanding or the characters' choices.</p>
