 <h2>Can silence do the work of dialogue?</h2>
 <p>Yes, when viewers know enough to interpret it. A woman discovers that her partner has lied, then continues an ordinary dinner conversation without mentioning the discovery. We watch what she notices, what she chooses not to say and how he responds. Silence becomes an action because withholding the truth is a decision.</p>
 <p>Without context, however, silence may simply be empty screen time. The writer must establish what the audience is waiting to learn.</p>
 <h2>How do you decide what to cut?</h2>
-<p>Robert McKee's approach to scene design offers a practical question: does the scene turn? A character might begin confident and leave doubtful, enter as an ally and depart as an opponent, or discover information that makes an earlier choice impossible.</p>
+<p>Robert McKee&#x27;s approach to scene design offers a practical question: does the scene turn? A character might begin confident and leave doubtful, enter as an ally and depart as an opponent, or discover information that makes an earlier choice impossible.</p>
 <p>If nothing changes, consider whether the scene belongs in the screenplay. If the change occurs halfway through, consider whether the remaining dialogue adds a necessary consequence. Conversely, if cutting a conversation removes the moment when trust breaks or a decision becomes inevitable, the cut has gone too far.</p>
 <p>Pacing is also a matter of contrast. A restrained exchange may be particularly effective after a crisis; a sudden interruption may have greater force when the audience has settled into a quieter rhythm.</p>
-<h1>What can screenwriters learn from their own viewing habits?</h1>
+<h2>What can screenwriters learn from their own viewing habits?</h2>
+<hr>
 <p>Notice what makes you continue watching. Is it fear for a character, the pleasure of solving a puzzle, the humour of an exchange, or the anticipation of a difficult choice? Different genres and audiences favour different combinations.</p>
 <p>Writers are often told to make a script faster. A more precise instruction is to make its dramatic purpose clearer. Some scenes should end almost as soon as they begin. Others deserve the time it takes for a person to realise that everything has changed.</p>
